Manager: So what did you find? Any signs that the signal could be from an intelligent species?
- Signal splits into even numbers.
	Manager: Interesting. I'll corroborate your findings with the others.
- Signal splits into odd numbers.
	Manager: Interesting. I'll corroborate your findings with the others.
- Signal splits into prime numbers.
	Manager: Great! That corroborates the other findings. It's also a strong indicator of an intelligent source for the signal.
- I have no idea.
	Manager: That's fine. That's why there are others in each team.
Manager: Based on how our team has divided the first signal, I believe the last part of the signal is made of 773 datapoints.
You: Weren't there more parts of the signal to analyze before the last portion?
Manager: The were several, and they're a slow build-up of ever increasingly challenging signals to guide us to the final solution.
- So shouldn't we do them in order?
- Aren't there some rules we're supposed to follow?
- That sounds easier than jumping ahead.
Manager: Each team operates independently, so nobody said we can't skip ahead if we're confident how to read the last one.
- Are we confident?
- I don't feel confident.
- Uhh...
Manager: I happen to know the next signal segments are guiding teams to transform the signals into their component frequencies with Discrete Fourier Transforms.
Manager: Similar to your cyclograph, the amplitude of each frequency defines the radius of an epicycle.
- A what?
- Umm...
- Oh, right that makes total sense.
Manager: Like spirographs inside of other spirographs, we can use these to draw any kind of shape.
Manager: I'm giving you a different toolset to process such a complex dataset.
Manager: So let's beat the other teams and be the first to see what the aliens wanted humanity to see before their civilization froze over!
=> END